equency is:\n\n\u0394f = f_infinity_final - f_infinity_initial \u2248 f_emitted (\u03a6_final - \u03a6_initial)/c\u00b2 \u2248 f_emitted \u0394\u03a6 / c\u00b2\n\nSo the relative change is \u0394f / f_emitted \u2248 \u0394\u03a6 / c\u00b2 \u2248 0.001 \u03a6_initial / c\u00b2\n\nBut \u03a6_initial is the gravitational potential at the satellite's original position, which is -GM/r_initial. Alternatively, since the satellite is in orbit, we can relate \u03a6_initial to the gravitational acceleration. Wait, gravitational potential \u03a6 = -GM/r. Gravitational acceleration g = GM/r\u00b2. So \u03a6 = -g r.\n\nTherefore, \u03a6_initial = -g_initial r_initial\n\nSimilarly, \u03a6_final = -g_final r_final\n\nBut g_final = g_initial + 0.002 g_initial = 1.002 g_initial\n\nAnd from before, r_final \u2248 0.999 r_initial\n\nTherefore, \u03a6_final = -1.002 g_initial * 0.999 r_initial \u2248 -1.002 * 0.999 g_initial r_initial \u2248 -1.001 g_initial r_initial\n\nBut \u03a6_initial = -g_initial r_initial, so \u03a6_final \u2248 1.001 \u03a6_initial\n\nTherefore, \u0394\u03a6 = \u03a6_final - \u03a6_initial \u2248 1.001 \u03a6_initial - \u03a6_initial = 0.001 \u03a6_initial\n\nSo substituting back, \u0394f/f \u2248 0.001 \u03a6_initial / c\u00b2\n\nBut \u03a6_initial = -g_initial r_initial, so:\n\n\u0394f/f \u2248 0.001 (-g_initial r_initial) / c\u00b2\n\nBut what is g_initial r_initial? For a satellite in circular orbit, the gravitational acceleration is equal to the centripetal acceleration: g = v\u00b2 / r. Also, orbital velocity v = sqrt(GM/r). So g = GM/r\u00b2. But how to express g r? g r = GM/r\u00b2 * r = GM/r. Wait, GM/r is equal to -\u03a6_initial. So \u03a6_initial = -GM/r = -g r. Therefore, g r = -\u03a6_initial. Wait, but we have \u0394f/f \u2248 0.001 (-g r) / c\u00b2 = 0.001 (- (-\u03a6_initial)) / c\u00b2 = 0.001 \u03a6_initial / c\u00b2. Hmm, this seems circular. Alternatively, since \u03a6_initial = -g_initial r_initial, then:\n\n\u0394f/f \u2248 0.001 (-g_init