r Frequency**:\n   - The energy levels of hydrogen are given by \\( E_n = -\\frac{13.6 \\, \\text{eV}}{n^2} \\).\n   - Transition from \\( n=2 \\) to \\( n=1 \\):  \n     \\( \\Delta E = 10.2 \\, \\text{eV} = 1.634 \\times 10^{-18} \\, \\text{J} \\).\n   - Angular frequency:  \n     \\( \\omega = \\frac{\\Delta E}{\\hbar} = \\frac{1.634 \\times 10^{-18} \\, \\text{J}}{1.055 \\times 10^{-34} \\, \\text{J\u00b7s}} \\approx 1.55 \\times 10^{16} \\, \\text{rad/s} \\).\n\n2. **Dipole Matrix Element**:\n   - Wavefunctions for \\( 1s \\) (\\( n=1, l=0 \\)) and \\( 2p \\) (\\( n=2, l=1 \\)) states.\n   - Radial integral \\( \\int_0^\\infty R_{1s}(r) r R_{2p}(r) r^2 dr \\) yields \\( \\frac{2^7 \\sqrt{2} a_0}{3^5} \\).\n   - Angular contribution gives \\( \\frac{1}{3} \\) when averaged over all orientations.\n   - Total \\( |\\langle f | \\mathbf{r} | i \\rangle|^2 \\approx 0.555 a_0^2 \\), where \\( a_0 = 0.529 \\times 10^{-10} \\, \\text{m} \\).\n\n3. **Einstein A Coefficient**:\n   - Formula:  \n     \\( A = \\frac{\\omega^3 e^2 |\\langle f | \\mathbf{r} | i \\rangle|^2}{3 \\pi \\epsilon_0 \\hbar c^3} \\).\n   - Substituting values:  \n     \\( A \\approx \\frac{(1.55 \\times 10^{16})^3 (1.602 \\times 10^{-19})^2 (0.555 \\times (0.529 \\times 10^{-10})^2)}{3 \\pi (8.854 \\times 10^{-12}) (1.055 \\times 10^{-34}) (3 \\times 10^8)^3} \\).\n   - Simplifying: \\( A \\approx 6.25 \\times 10^8 \\, \\text{s}^{-1} \\).\n\n4. **Lifetime Calculation**:\n   - Lifetime \\( \\tau = \\frac{1}{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{6.25 \\times 10^8} \\, \\text{s} \\approx 1.6 \\, \\text{ns} \\).\n\n**Final Answer**:  \nThe lifetime of the first excited state (2p) of a hydrogen atom is approximately **1.6 nanoseconds**.", "ground_truth_solution": null, "domain": "physics", "source": "camelai_physics", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A helium atom in an excited state has a lifetime of 2.0 nanoseconds. Calculate the probability that the helium atom will decay in the next 1.0 nanose